#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1020.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093288} :activity/id #uuid "fc499402-7fb4-528a-a437-297d7d142802" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050598 :topic/id #uuid "5761edb2-80f5-563f-ad9c-bf729c12d152" :topic/math-academy-id 1004} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093288 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093400 :tutorial/content "Consider the following geometric sequence: \n\n$$\n3,\\quad 6,\\quad 12,\\quad 24, \\quad \\ldots\n$$\n\n \n\nSuppose we want to find the first term that exceeds $3\\,000\\,000.$ We can do this by creating and solving an inequality.\n\nFirst, let's construct a formula for the $n$th term of the sequence. The first term of the sequence is $a_1=3,$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr = \\dfrac 6 3 = 2.\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the sequence can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =3\\cdot {2}^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be greater than $3\\,000\\,000.$ So, we can set up and solve the corresponding inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & >3\\,000\\,000 \\\\ 3\\cdot {2}^{n-1} & >3\\,000\\,000 \\\\ {2}^{n-1} & >1\\,000\\,000 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}({2}^{n-1}) & >\\operatorname{log}(1\\,000\\,000) \\\\ (n-1)\\operatorname{log}(2) & >\\operatorname{log}(1\\,000\\,000) \\\\ n-1 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}(1\\,000\\,000)}{\\operatorname{log}(2)} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}(1\\,000\\,000)}{\\operatorname{log}(2)}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\log (1\\,000\\,000)} {\\log (2)} + 1 \\approx 20.932.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $21$st term is the first term of the sequence that exceeds $3\\,000\\,000.$\n\nTo check our answer, we can easily verify that $a_{20} < 3\\, 000\\, 000$ and $a_{21} > 3\\, 000\\, 000\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{20} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{20-1}=3\\cdot {2}^{19}=1\\,572\\,864<3\\,000\\,000 \\\\ {a}_{21} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{21-1}=3\\cdot {2}^{20}=3\\,145\\,728>3\\,000\\,000\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $a_{21}$ is indeed the first term that exceeds $3\\,000\\,000.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "9a55e4c5-cd46-5a39-97aa-f29fe4e37185" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2886 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "7ae31a98-46a1-5e73-a782-739c76a5378c" :step/math-academy-id 466081 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093289}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093289 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092360 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092020 :question/id #uuid "c3aac9bd-c462-5664-a196-278a9671fbbb" :question/math-academy-id "e-4243"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e3b20b5-9427-538d-9392-aa137b5d8126" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048553} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050067}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092940} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092941} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092942} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292719} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294937} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294938} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294942} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360806}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Term Exceeding a Given Value"} :step/id #uuid "06291b9a-e86c-591b-9600-116684c83888" :step/math-academy-id 466082 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093290}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093290 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092361 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092021 :question/id #uuid "4af09b88-5f57-572f-ae64-bfe64e94450d" :question/math-academy-id "e-4244"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "43dcbb7e-ba21-506f-a706-bfb28371e0a9" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092798} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092943} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290354} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294943} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294946} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326771} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326772} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335931}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Term Smaller Than a Given Value"} :step/id #uuid "5d2